      "turn": 1,
      "user": "Can hempcrete hold up my roof, and is it recognized by U.S. residential code?",
      "assistant": "Hempcrete is a fantastic insulating material, but it cannot carry structural loads like a roof on its own. Instead, it is used as a non-load-bearing infill wall around a structural frame—such as timber, steel, or post-and-beam—that actually supports the weight of the roof. \n\nRegarding U.S. residential building codes, hemp-lime (hempcrete) is not yet universally covered under standard prescriptive provisions in the International Residential Code (IRC). However, builders frequently gain approval through jurisdiction-specific alternative methods, engineering letters, or Appendix pathways where available, though local code officials always have final authority.\n\nTo help you plan further, roughly what total wall area (in square feet) and wall thickness are you considering for your project?"
